---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/attentive-alternative';
const title = 'Attentive alternatives for Shopify: replacement decisions';
const desc = 'Plan the ownership of acquisition, email, SMS and order notifications before replacing a connected program.';
const faqs = [{"question": "Should a small Shopify store automatically leave Attentive?", "answer": "No. Compare the actual workflows, staffing, service use and matched quote. Store size alone does not establish fit."}, {"question": "Can order updates and marketing move together?", "answer": "Only after their distinct purposes, permission states and notification ownership are mapped and tested. Avoid duplicate Shopify and replacement notifications."}, {"question": "Can I keep Attentive SMS while changing email?", "answer": "Scope the email handover separately, verify integration and suppression boundaries, and confirm the account arrangements. Do not assume a split stack preserves every journey."}, {"question": "How long does an exit take?", "answer": "Use confirmed notice, export and sender requirements plus successful acceptance checks. No fixed duration applies to every contract or store."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Attentive alternatives for Shopify</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl">Plan the ownership of acquisition, email, SMS and order notifications before replacing a connected program.</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">
      <h2>Start with what Attentive already provides</h2>
      <p>Attentive supports Shopify-connected messaging rather than only cart texts. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/9555427119508-Send-Shopify-Transactional-and-Promotional-Journeys">Shopify journey documentation</a> describes order and shipment triggers and distinguishes transactional confirmation from promotional post-purchase messaging. Keeping those jobs connected can be valuable when one team operates both channels.</p>
      <p>The same guide warns about duplicate journeys and corresponding Shopify notifications. A replacement must account for that existing routing. Do not infer poor email performance, a minimum store size or a universal contract price from the platform category. Stay if the current workflow and service meet your needs; investigate a move when a specific requirement or matched quote justifies the handover.</p>
      <h2>Build a responsibility map before a shortlist</h2>
      <p>List capture units, keywords, signup incentives, SMS campaigns, email journeys, order notifications and connected support tools. For each, record event source, purpose, permission requirement, templates, dynamic fields, queue owner and escalation contact. Identify which tasks your account team performs and which your staff would inherit. Confirm rights to templates/assets and access to records rather than assuming every configuration exports.</p>
      <p>A candidate for unified email/SMS must reproduce your branching and exclusions. A split stack needs an explicit boundary for shared offers, identity and withdrawals. Ask any candidate, including Sequenzy for an email role, to demonstrate that boundary before quoting savings. For a narrower managed SMS/capture change, use the <a class="link" href="/alternatives/recart-alternative">Recart alternative guide</a>; that cart acceptance job does not cover order-notification ownership.</p>
      <h2>Proposed acceptance job: a gift and refill store</h2>
      <p>This is a proposed fixture, not an observed migration. Use a gift purchaser who accepts order updates but declines promotional SMS, a marketing subscriber buying a refill, and an existing subscriber with an open support ticket. Trace order ID, customer identity, channel/purpose permission, product and gift marker through each branch. Order confirmation must not grant marketing permission; the gift purchaser must not enter purchaser-as-user replenishment by default.</p>
      <p>Deliver a partial shipment, cancellation and refund event and verify order links, current status and template variables. Decide which Shopify notification remains active before enabling a corresponding replacement. Attentive documents plan-dependent Shopify notification controls; confirm the actual store controls rather than disabling everything. Keep necessary service messages available while marketing is paused.</p>
      <p>For the opted-in refill buyer, propose educational email after purchase and a later replenishment invitation grounded in usage data. Recheck current purchase, withdrawal, support status and channel frequency at dispatch. Preserve separately opted-in VIP launch access, but do not let a launch override a purchase exit or support exclusion.</p>
      <h2>Prove replay and suppression across the boundary</h2>
      <p>Export permission provenance with sender, purpose, disclosure/version, source and timestamp; reconcile current email and SMS withdrawals independently. Confirm the destination can represent transactional-only and marketing records distinctly. Count accepted, rejected, duplicate and suppressed records and resolve discrepancies before activating the relevant journey.</p>
      <p>Replay the same purchase and shipment event, deliver them out of order and retry after an acknowledgement timeout. Reconcile destination acceptance IDs before retrying an unknown send outcome. Use event/order/purpose identity so a duplicate integration event cannot issue another confirmation or promotion. A delayed purchase must cancel queued cart recovery; a later withdrawal must cancel pending promotional steps in either channel.</p>
      <p>Test STOP/HELP and free-text replies through the real intended inbox path, with a named operator for failures. Verify local-time quiet-hour handling and postponed-send eligibility for each message class; do not assume identical rules for service and marketing messages. Recheck keyword and form disclosures against the actual new journeys. These acceptance checks do not constitute a legal compliance certification.</p>
      <h2>Exit and rollback are part of the replacement</h2>
      <p>Confirm notice terms, export access, sender ownership and any port eligibility with both providers. Inventory sending domains, authentication, credentials, capture scripts and creative/service dependencies. Set cutover from confirmed prerequisites, not a fixed enterprise timeline. Archive configuration and permission evidence with appropriate access controls before removing access.</p>
      <p>Move one defined purpose at a time. Freeze old entry points and cancel old queues before assigning that purpose to the new operator. Record cutover event IDs and accepted sends. If a failure forces rollback, pause replacement queues, reconcile accepted messages and fresh purchases/withdrawals, then resume only eligible unsent work. Restoring old templates must not restore obsolete permission or duplicate order notifications.</p>
      <h2>Compare complete work and contribution</h2>
      <p>Use matched ordinary and peak workloads for email profiles/sends, SMS destinations and segments, MMS, carrier and sender charges, platform/modules, creative and operating labor. Include replacement of services your team actually uses. A lower subscription can require more staff; neither an enterprise label nor a small store proves poor fit.</p>
      <p>Illustrative contribution: a $100 basket less $45 goods and $10 other variable costs leaves $45. A $25 incentive leaves $20 before messaging. At twenty incremental orders and $300 program cost, contribution is $100; thirty orders and $450 cost leave $150. These are assumptions, not quotes or merchant outcomes. Compare opt-outs and reply backlog by journey as well as revenue. Use a suitable holdout where feasible; attributed orders alone do not measure causal lift.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
